      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Sealer is dry in hours and hard in weeks. Every tire mark we have ever been asked about happened somewhere in the gap between those two, and most of them were gone by the time the coating caught up.
        </p>

        <h2 id="why" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. Why Sealer Scuffs: The Mechanics
        </h2>

        <p>
          Driveway sealer, whether asphalt emulsion, coal tar or acrylic, cures in two stages. The first is evaporation: the water carrier leaves, the surface turns from brown to black and stops being tacky, and this is what the pail means by "dry" in four to twenty-four hours. The second stage is much slower. The asphalt or resin particles that were suspended in the water have to fuse together into a continuous, tough film, and that coalescence continues for days and, in cool weather or with a thick coat, for weeks. A sealed drive at 24 hours is a skin over a still-soft coating. It will take a car rolling straight across it. It will not reliably take a tire pivoting in place.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power-Steering Scuffs</h3>
        <p>
          This is the cause of the overwhelming majority of marks. When you turn the steering wheel while the car is stationary, or nearly so, the front tires do not roll; they twist against the surface, and the contact patch of each tire, roughly the size of a hand, is dragged sideways across the coating under the full weight of the front axle. Power steering makes this effortless, so people do it without thinking, particularly when backing into a garage or manoeuvring around a second car. On a fully cured coating it does nothing. On a coating that is still soft, it shears the top layer of sealer and drags it into a swirl, exposing the slightly different surface underneath. The result is a lighter, sometimes glossier, crescent or swirl exactly where the front wheels sat.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Heat Makes Everything Worse</h3>
        <p>
          Asphalt-based coatings are thermoplastic. They soften as they warm, and a black driveway in direct July sun can reach 130 to 150 degrees Fahrenheit at the surface. A sealer that is perfectly firm at 70 degrees can be soft enough to take a fingerprint at 140, and a car that has just come off the highway is parking hot tires onto it. This is why marks appear in summer far more than in spring or autumn, why they cluster around the parking spot rather than the length of the drive, and why a coating that survived its first week can still scuff in a heatwave a month later. In our experience the first summer after sealing is when nearly all of it happens; by the second season the coating has hardened enough that only genuinely abusive dry-steering leaves a trace.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why Coal Tar and Emulsion Differ Slightly</h3>
        <p>
          Coal tar sealers tend to cure harder and are a little less prone to hot-weather scuffing once they have fully set, but they are notably soft in their first few weeks and are the coating that most often produces the swirl marks contractors get called back about. Asphalt emulsion is softer throughout its life and more thermoplastic, so it is slightly more prone to summer marking, but the marks also tend to blend faster because the surrounding coating weathers and dulls to match. Acrylics cure hardest and fastest and scuff least, which is one reason they are marketed for high-turn areas. None of the three is immune. Our <a href="/coal-tar-vs-asphalt-emulsion-sealer/">coal tar vs asphalt emulsion</a> comparison covers the broader differences.
        </p>

        <h2 id="types" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Reading the Mark: Scuff, Smear or Pickup
        </h2>

        <p>
          Before deciding what to do, look closely at what kind of mark you have, because the three common types have different causes and different remedies.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Lighter Scuff</h3>
        <p>
          Most tire marks on fresh sealer are lighter than the surrounding coating, not darker, which surprises people who expected a black rubber streak. What has happened is that the top skin of the sealer, which has cured further and is slightly glossy, has been sheared off or smeared aside, exposing sealer underneath that has cured less and reflects light differently. It can also be that the fine clay and filler particles at the surface have been pushed around. The mark is in the coating, not on it, which is why it does not wash off, and also why it fades: as the exposed layer catches up in cure and the whole surface weathers, the contrast disappears. This is the harmless kind.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Black Smear</h3>
        <p>
          A genuinely black, sometimes slightly shiny streak is rubber transferred from the tire onto the coating, or occasionally a mix of rubber and road grime. It happens when a hot tire sits on or slides across a surface that is just tacky enough to grab rubber, and it is more common with softer performance tires and in extreme heat. This mark is on the coating rather than in it, and in most cases it scrubs off.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pickup and Tearing</h3>
        <p>
          The third type is the one to take seriously. If the tire has lifted the coating, so that you can see bare grey asphalt in the tire's footprint, or if there are curls or flakes of sealer at the edges of the mark, the sealer was either far too under-cured to be driven on or it never bonded to the surface in the first place. Pickup on day one is a curing problem and is usually limited to the spot. Pickup after a week, or pickup that spreads as more cars use the drive, is an adhesion failure and belongs in the section on <a href="#failing">when marks mean the sealer is failing</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="causes" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. The Application Causes
        </h2>

        <p>
          Dry-steering on soft sealer is the trigger, but how badly a drive marks, and how long it stays vulnerable, comes down to how the sealer went on. On the driveways we have sealed and the ones we have been asked to diagnose, the same handful of application decisions account for nearly every bad case.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Applied Too Thick</h3>
        <p>
          This is the big one. A thick coat of sealer skins over on top while the material underneath stays wet, and that trapped moisture can take a week or more to work its way out. The surface feels dry, the car goes on, and the skin shears over the soft layer beneath like the crust on a crème brûlée. Sealer should go on thin, at the coverage rate the manufacturer states, and if you want more protection the answer is a second thin coat after the first has cured, not a heavier first coat. Our guide on <a href="/how-many-coats-of-driveway-sealer/">how many coats of driveway sealer</a> explains why two thin beats one thick every time. If your drive was hand-squeegeed by someone trying to use up the pail, thickness is almost certainly your problem.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">No Sand or Aggregate</h3>
        <p>
          Contractors nearly always mix silica sand into sealer, at somewhere between two and five pounds per gallon, and homeowners nearly never do. Sand does two things for scuffing. It gives the cured film a fine texture that breaks up the smooth sliding shear a pivoting tire applies, so the load is carried on thousands of hard grains rather than on a continuous soft film. And it stiffens the coating overall. A sanded coating still marks if it is driven on too soon, but it marks less, and it stops marking sooner. Our piece on <a href="/sand-additive-for-driveway-sealer/">sand additive for driveway sealer</a> covers the how and how much.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Low-Solids or Over-Diluted Product</h3>
        <p>
          Sealers vary a great deal in solids content, the proportion of the pail that is actual coating rather than water. Premium products from the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealer</a> lists sit at the higher end; bargain pails and some contractor mixes are considerably thinner. A low-solids sealer leaves a thinner, weaker film that softens more readily in heat. Contractors also dilute concentrate with water to make it sprayable, and an over-diluted mix has the same effect. If a contractor's job scuffs badly in its first summer, ask what the mix ratio was.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sealed in the Wrong Weather</h3>
        <p>
          Sealer applied late in the day, in high humidity, on a cool evening or ahead of overnight dew cures slowly, and slow-curing sealer stays scuffable for longer. Conversely, sealer applied in extreme heat skins over so fast that the coat beneath never releases its water properly. The window in <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">what temperature to seal a driveway</a>, roughly 55 to 85 degrees and falling humidity, exists for exactly this reason.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Driven on Too Soon</h3>
        <p>
          The 24-hour figure on the pail is a minimum in good conditions for a car rolling straight on and off. It is not a promise that the coating will resist a front tire pivoting under a 4,000-pound SUV at noon in August. More on the realistic timeline in the prevention section below and in our guide on <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">how long driveway sealer takes to dry</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="fixes" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The Fixes, in Order
        </h2>

        <p>
          Start at the top of this list and only move down if the previous step has not worked. Most people never need to get past the first two.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix 1: Leave It Alone</h3>
        <p>
          For lighter scuffs, this is the genuine, honest answer, and it is the one people least want to hear. The coating is still curing, the scuffed area is curing too, and over the next two to six weeks of sun, rain and traffic the contrast between them will shrink until you cannot find the mark without looking for it. We have seen swirls that looked appalling on day two become invisible by week four with no intervention at all. Anything you do to a two-day-old coating, scrubbing, wetting, adding more product, risks making the mark worse or creating a new one. Give it a month. Set a reminder if it helps.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix 2: Soap and Water for Rubber Transfer</h3>
        <p>
          Black smears that sit on the coating usually come off with warm water, a squirt of dish soap and a stiff-bristled deck brush. Wet the area, scrub in the direction of the mark, rinse with a garden hose, and let it dry before judging. If that does not shift it, a driveway degreaser or one of the products in our <a href="/best-driveway-cleaner/">best driveway cleaner</a> guide, applied per the label, will lift heavier rubber deposits. Do not use solvents, which soften the sealer, and do not pressure wash a coating that is less than about a month old; a zero-degree tip on fresh sealer strips it straight down to asphalt. Wait for the coating to fully harden before any aggressive cleaning.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix 3: Touch-Up Coat</h3>
        <p>
          If a mark has not blended after a month, or if the tire has exposed bare asphalt, touch it up. Clean the area and let it dry completely. Stir the same sealer you used originally and brush on a thin coat, extending well beyond the mark in every direction and feathering the edge to nothing. The temptation is to dab on a good thick layer to be sure of covering it; resist that, because a thick patch cures dark and glossy, stands proud, and is far more visible than the scuff was. Two thin passes, if needed, a day apart. Then keep cars off it for the full 48 hours and do not dry-steer on it for a week. Touch-ups done this way disappear into the surrounding coating once it all weathers together.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix 4: A Second Full Coat</h3>
        <p>
          If the drive is peppered with marks from a summer of parking, or if the first coat was thin and patchy anyway, a second full coat is the cleanest fix and it is what most drives should have had in the first place. Wait until the first coat is thoroughly cured, at least a week and ideally longer, clean the surface, and apply a thin second coat with sand added. Then, crucially, give it the extended cure time described below before parking on it, or you will be back where you started.
        </p>

        <h2 id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Preventing It Next Time
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Extend the Cure Before Parking</h3>
        <p>
          Treat the label's 24 hours as the point at which you can walk on it and, in mild weather, roll a car straight across it. For parking and turning, wait 48 hours as a minimum and 72 hours in summer heat, with heavy vehicles, or after a thick or sanded coat. If the forecast is for a stretch of 90-degree days, plan the sealing for a week when you can leave the car on the street for three days. That single change eliminates most of the scuffing we are asked about.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Change How You Turn</h3>
        <p>
          The habit that saves your sealer is simple: keep the car moving while you turn the wheel. Rolling tires do not shear the coating; pivoting ones do. Coming into the drive, start turning before you have stopped. Backing into the garage, let the car creep as you steer rather than stopping, cranking the wheel, then moving. Do not sit stationary and turn the wheel to straighten up. It takes about a week to make this automatic, and it is the difference between a coating that scuffs every hot afternoon and one that does not. Tell anyone else who parks there, because the marks are always made by the person who was not told.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Add Sand</h3>
        <p>
          Next time, mix silica sand into the sealer. Two to three pounds per gallon is a sensible starting point for a residential drive; more gives more texture but also a duller finish. Keep it stirred while you work because it settles fast. Sanded sealer resists scuffing, adds traction on a wet slope, and hides minor surface irregularities. The main drawback is a rougher look, which most people stop noticing within a week.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Apply Thin, Twice</h3>
        <p>
          Two thin coats, the second applied only once the first has fully cured, produce a harder, more uniform film than one heavy coat and cure through far faster. This is standard advice for a reason. If you are applying with a squeegee, pull the excess off rather than leaving it to level; if you are using a roller or spray, resist going back over areas that look thin until the first coat has dried and you can see where it actually is. The <a href="/brush-vs-roller-vs-squeegee-driveway-sealer/">brush vs roller vs squeegee</a> guide covers how each tool affects film thickness.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pick the Weather</h3>
        <p>
          Seal in the morning of a warm, dry day with low humidity and no rain for 48 hours, so the coating gets a full day of sun and a dry night to cure before the next hot afternoon. Avoid sealing in a heatwave if you can; if you cannot, do it early, and keep cars off it for the full three days.
        </p>

        <h2 id="failing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. When Marks Mean the Sealer Is Failing
        </h2>

        <p>
          Everything above assumes the coating is fundamentally sound and simply too young. Sometimes the tire is just the first thing to reveal that the sealer was never going to work. The distinguishing signs are worth knowing because the remedy is completely different.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Still Tacky After a Week</h3>
        <p>
          A coating that is still soft or sticky to the touch seven days on, in reasonable weather, is not curing. The usual causes are a coat far too thick, sealer applied over a damp surface or over a surface that had just been pressure washed, high humidity throughout the cure window, or, occasionally, a bad or frozen pail. Tire marks on this coating are a symptom, not the problem. Our guide on <a href="/driveway-sealer-still-sticky/">driveway sealer that is still sticky</a> goes through the causes and what can be rescued.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rolling Up or Peeling in the Tire Path</h3>
        <p>
          If the tire is lifting the sealer in sheets or the coating is flaking away along the wheel tracks, the sealer did not bond. It was applied over dirt, dust, oil, pollen or old sealer that was itself letting go, or the drive was too new and still shedding oils, or it went on over a surface that had been sealed so many times there was nothing left to bond to. No amount of waiting fixes an adhesion failure. The loose material has to come off and the surface has to be properly cleaned before recoating, which is covered in <a href="/driveway-sealer-peeling/">driveway sealer peeling</a> and <a href="/how-to-clean-asphalt-driveway-before-sealing/">how to clean an asphalt driveway before sealing</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Scuffing Every Summer, Years On</h3>
        <p>
          A coating that marks under normal parking every hot season, three years after application, is either a very low-solids product or has been built up so thick over repeated resealings that the accumulated layers never fully harden. Drives that have been sealed every single year for a decade often have a soft, rubbery build-up that scuffs, cracks and holds tire marks permanently. The answer there is to stop resealing on a calendar and follow the guidance in <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal your driveway</a>, which for most drives is every two to four years, not annually.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Contractor Jobs</h3>
        <p>
          If a contractor sealed the drive and it scuffed badly within its first week despite you following their instructions, that is a reasonable callback. Most reputable outfits will touch up power-steering marks once without argument in the first month; it is a known issue in the trade and it costs them a few minutes. What they cannot do is prevent marks made by a car dry-steering on the coating at 48 hours in July, so be honest about what happened.
        </p>

        <h2 id="new-asphalt" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          7. Tire Marks on New Asphalt
        </h2>

        <p>
          A related question we get, from people who have just had a drive paved rather than sealed: why is the brand-new asphalt showing tire marks and scuffs? The mechanism is similar and the answer is even more firmly "leave it alone".
        </p>

        <p>
          Fresh hot-mix asphalt is soft. The binder has not yet oxidised and the surface stays pliable for months, longer in a hot summer, and it will take scuffs, indentations from jack stands and kickstands, and power-steering swirls from any tire that pivots on it. New asphalt also carries a thin surface film of light oils from the mix that shows every mark. None of this indicates a problem with the paving. Over the first season the surface cures and hardens, the sheen dulls, and the marks weather out; by the following spring most are gone.
        </p>

        <p>
          The precautions are the same as for sealer, only for longer: avoid dry-steering, do not park in exactly the same spot every day for the first few weeks in hot weather, keep trailers and anything on a narrow steel foot off it, and do not put a sealer over it to hide the marks. New asphalt should not be sealed for at least six months and usually a year, because it needs to shed those surface oils and harden first, and sealing early traps them and gives you a coating that never cures. Our guide on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> has the timeline. If the marks are still bothering you after that first year, the first coat of sealer covers them completely.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Treating the 24-hour figure as a cure time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Twenty-four hours is dry, not hard. Parking and turning want 48 to 72 hours, and longer in summer heat.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Turning the wheel with the car stopped</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Dry-steering under power assistance is what makes the swirl. Keep rolling while you steer and the coating never sees the shear.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Scrubbing or pressure washing a fresh scuff</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A two-day-old coating cannot take it. You will replace a mark that would have faded with a bare patch that will not.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Dabbing on a thick touch-up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A heavy spot of fresh sealer cures glossy and dark and shows far more than the scuff did. Thin, feathered, and patient.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skipping the sand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Contractors sand their sealer for a reason. A textured film shrugs off pivoting tires that a smooth one records.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are there tire marks on my newly sealed driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Almost always because the sealer was still soft when a tire turned on it. Sealer is dry to the touch in hours but takes days to weeks to fully harden, and a tire pivoting under power steering on a hot day drags the top layer of coating sideways. The result is a lighter scuff in the shape of the tire's footprint, often with a swirl to it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Will tire marks on driveway sealer go away on their own?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Most light scuffs do. As the coating continues to cure over the following two to six weeks and weathers under sun and rain, the difference between the scuffed and unscuffed areas fades and the marks blend in. Deeper marks that have exposed the asphalt underneath, or that have peeled the coating, will not.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I wait to drive on a sealed driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The label usually says 24 hours. In summer heat, with a heavy vehicle, or if you will need to turn the wheels while parking, we recommend 48 to 72 hours. Sealer applied on a hot week can stay soft enough to scuff for several days beyond the point where it feels dry.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I remove black rubber marks from a sealed driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Black smears are rubber transferred from the tire onto the coating rather than damage to it. Scrub with warm water, a squirt of dish soap and a stiff deck brush, then rinse. A degreaser-based driveway cleaner helps on stubborn ones. Avoid solvents and pressure washing on a coating less than a month old.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sand in driveway sealer stop tire marks?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It helps considerably. Silica sand mixed into the sealer gives the cured film a textured surface that resists the smooth sliding shear a pivoting tire applies, and it adds traction. It is standard practice for contractors and worth doing on any drive where cars turn in tight.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my driveway sealer scuff every summer even after it cured?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Coal tar and asphalt emulsion sealers soften in extreme heat, particularly in their first season, and a black surface at 130 to 150 degrees Fahrenheit is soft enough to scuff under a dry-steered tire. If it recurs year after year the coating was probably applied too thick or is a low-solids product. Rolling while turning and adding sand next time both help.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I touch up tire marks on driveway sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, if they have not faded after a month or have exposed bare asphalt. Clean the area, let it dry, and brush on a thin coat of the same sealer, feathering it out well past the mark. A thick dab will stand out more than the scuff did. Or wait and apply a second full coat, which hides everything.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do tire marks mean the sealer is failing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Usually not. Scuffs on a coating still curing are cosmetic. Sealer that is still tacky after a week, that rolls up under a tire in sheets, or that peels off in flakes along the tire path is a different problem, typically caused by applying over a dirty or damp surface, over old failing sealer, or in a coat far too thick.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Related Driveway Sealer Guides
        </h2>

        <ul>
          <li><a href="/driveway-sealer-still-sticky/">Driveway Sealer Still Sticky?</a> — when soft sealer is a curing failure rather than a waiting game.</li>
